<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_create.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-create">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-create">
  <primary>CREATE</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE</refname>
  <refpurpose>add the vertices and edges of a pattern to the graph</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> [, [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> ] ...

MATCH <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable>
CREATE [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> [, [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> ] ...
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-create-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE</command> writes its pattern into the graph. The pattern syntax is the one
   <xref linkend="cypher-match"/> reads with, described in <xref linkend="cypher-path"/>, but
   every element is built rather than looked for.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE</command> always creates. It does not check whether an identical vertex or
   edge is already there. To create only what is missing, use <xref linkend="cypher-merge"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   An element that names a variable already in scope is reused, not rebuilt. That is how an
   edge is attached to vertices a <command>MATCH</command> found: the endpoints are bound
   variables, so only the edge is new. An element that names a new variable, or none, is
   created. One pattern can therefore create a vertex and its edge in one step.
  </para>

  <para>
   Every element needs enough information to be built. An edge needs exactly one label and a
   direction. A vertex without a label becomes a vertex of the graph's base label
   <literal>ag_vertex</literal>. Labels have to exist already, unless
   <varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> is on, in which case they are created implicitly.
  </para>

  <para>
   A property map may be written inline, as in <literal>{title: 'Tenet'}</literal>, supplied as
   a query parameter, or supplied whole with <literal>=</literal> from an expression that
   yields a <type>jsonb</type> object. That last form is how a whole table row becomes a
   property bag. See <xref linkend="cypher-load"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE</command> runs once per incoming row. With no clause in front of it, it runs
   once. It may lead a query, follow another clause, and end one. Add a
   <xref linkend="cypher-return"/> to see what it built.
  </para>

  <para>
   <xref linkend="cypher-insert"/> is an exact synonym, the spelling ISO/IEC 39075 uses.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-create-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Optional name bound to the whole path the pattern created. Returning it yields a
      <type>graphpath</type> value listing the new vertices and edges in order.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A vertex pattern or a path pattern in <xref linkend="cypher-match"/> syntax. Several may
      be listed, separated by commas. They are created in order, and a later pattern may use a
      variable an earlier one bound.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-create-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Create one vertex:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:movie {title: 'Tenet', year: 2020, genre: 'Action'});

UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
   A graph write reports the number of graph elements it touched under the
   <literal>UPDATE</literal> tag, whatever the clause was.
  </para>

  <para>
   Bind a variable to see what was built:
<programlisting>
CREATE (p:person {name: 'John David Washington', born: 1984})
RETURN p.name AS person, p.born AS born;

         person          | born
-------------------------+------
 "John David Washington" | 1984
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Find both endpoints first, then create only the edge:
<programlisting>
MATCH (d:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'}), (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
CREATE (d)-[r:directed]-&gt;(m)
RETURN d.name AS director, label(r) AS edge, m.title AS film;

      director       |    edge    |  film
---------------------+------------+---------
 "Christopher Nolan" | "directed" | "Tenet"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   <literal>d</literal> and <literal>m</literal> are already bound, so they are reused. Had the
   pattern written <literal>(d:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})</literal> again inside the
   <command>CREATE</command>, a second Christopher Nolan would have been created.
  </para>

  <para>
   Anything in the pattern that is not already bound is built, so a vertex and its edge can be
   created together:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
CREATE (a:person {name: 'Elizabeth Debicki', born: 1990})-[r:acted_in {role: 'Kat'}]-&gt;(m)
RETURN a.name AS actor, r.role AS role, m.title AS film;

        actor        | role  |  film
---------------------+-------+---------
 "Elizabeth Debicki" | "Kat" | "Tenet"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Patterns separated by commas are built in one clause and may share variables:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'John David Washington'}), (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role: 'The Protagonist'}]-&gt;(m),
       (m)-[:related_to {weight: 2}]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'Memento', year: 2000, genre: 'Thriller'});

UPDATE 3
</programlisting>
   Three elements: one <literal>acted_in</literal> edge, one <literal>related_to</literal> edge
   and the <literal>Memento</literal> vertex. <literal>Tenet</literal> now has four edges:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})-[r]-(x)
RETURN label(r) AS edge, label(x) AS other, coalesce(x.name, x.title) AS name
ORDER BY edge, name;

     edge     |  other   |          name
--------------+----------+-------------------------
 "acted_in"   | "person" | "Elizabeth Debicki"
 "acted_in"   | "person" | "John David Washington"
 "directed"   | "person" | "Christopher Nolan"
 "related_to" | "movie"  | "Memento"
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Nothing about <command>CREATE</command> is idempotent:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:person {name: 'Duplicate', born: 1}), (:person {name: 'Duplicate', born: 1});

UPDATE 2
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Duplicate'}) RETURN count(*) AS rows;

 rows
------
 2
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Running the first statement again would make four. Use <xref linkend="cypher-merge"/>, or a
   unique constraint, when a key has to stay unique.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-create-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   A vertex id is assigned from the label's own sequence, so the number a new vertex gets
   depends on what has been created in that label before. Read it with
   <literal>id(v)</literal>. Do not depend on a particular value.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>CREATE ()</literal> is legal and produces a vertex of the base label
   <literal>ag_vertex</literal>. Such a vertex is invisible to every
   <literal>MATCH (n:label)</literal> and easy to lose track of. Give every vertex a label.
   Naming the base label explicitly is refused:
   <literal>CREATE (:ag_vertex)</literal> raises
   <literal>specifying default label is not allowed</literal>, and
   <literal>CREATE ()-[:ag_edge]-&gt;()</literal> raises
   <literal>cannot create edge on default label</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   An edge pattern with no label, or with more than one, raises
   <literal>only one relationship type is allowed for CREATE</literal>. An undirected edge
   pattern raises <literal>only directed relationships are allowed in CREATE</literal>. A
   pattern whose single element is a variable already bound raises
   <literal>there must be at least one relationship</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-create-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 names this statement <command>INSERT</command>, in section 13.2.
   AgensGraph accepts both spellings for the same clause and builds the same tree for each, so
   the standard conformance of the clause is described under <xref linkend="cypher-insert"/>.
   The SQL statements that begin with the same word, such as <command>CREATE GRAPH</command>
   and <command>CREATE VLABEL</command>, are separate statements and are unaffected.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE</command> is the openCypher spelling, and AgensGraph accepts openCypher
   create patterns unchanged.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

